# Environment variables for EDI.
# Copy this file to `.env` and fill in what you need.
#
# Everything here is read by the Python backend. The frontend holds no
# credentials of its own -- it talks only to the backend, which is the only
# thing that reaches a model or a database.
#
# There is no required variable. With nothing set at all, EDI stores workspaces
# in a local file and looks for a model on http://localhost:11434, which is
# where Ollama listens. Fill things in to point it somewhere else.


# --- The model -------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Nothing here is required. EDI looks at the machine it is running on and uses
# what it finds -- models Ollama has pulled, Claude if the CLI is signed in, a
# key already in your environment -- and the dropdown in the chat box switches
# between them without a restart. That choice is saved in .edi-data/model.json
# and wins over the variables below, which is what makes the dropdown feel like
# a setting rather than a suggestion. Clear it from the same dropdown.
#
# What is here is for pinning a deployment to one model, where nobody is going
# to be clicking anything. Which model you pick determines the quality of the
# answers -- `python backend/check_model.py` tests whichever is configured and
# tells you whether it can do the job.

# One of: google | openai | anthropic | groq | ollama | claude |
# openai-compatible. `anthropic` and `claude` reach the same models; the first
# wants an API key, the second uses the Claude Code CLI you are already signed
# in to. Unset means "work it out", in this order: a choice saved from the
# picker, then this variable, then GOOGLE_API_KEY on its own, then the first
# thing on this machine that answers.
# EDI_LLM_PROVIDER=ollama

# The model itself. Every provider has a sensible default, so this is only
# needed to choose a different one.
#   google             gemini-2.5-flash
#   openai             gpt-4o-mini
#   anthropic          claude-sonnet-5
#   groq               llama-3.3-70b-versatile
#   ollama             qwen2.5-coder:7b
#   claude             sonnet  (or opus, haiku, fable, or a full model id)
#   openai-compatible  no default -- you must name one
# EDI_LLM_MODEL=llama3.1:8b

# The API key. EDI_LLM_API_KEY works for any provider; the conventional
# per-provider names below are also read, so a key you already have in your
# environment is picked up without renaming.
#   GOOGLE_API_KEY / GEMINI_API_KEY   https://aistudio.google.com/apikey
#   OPENAI_API_KEY
#   ANTHROPIC_API_KEY
#   GROQ_API_KEY
# Ollama needs none, and neither does `claude`: it runs the CLI you have
# already signed in to, and never reads or copies its credentials.
#
# A key typed into the model dropdown instead is written to
# .edi-data/model.json on this machine and is never sent back to the browser.
# It takes precedence over anything set here.
GOOGLE_API_KEY=
# EDI_LLM_API_KEY=

# Where to reach the model. Only for ollama (default http://localhost:11434)
# and openai-compatible, which is how you point EDI at OpenRouter, LM Studio,
# vLLM, Together, or llama.cpp's server.
# EDI_LLM_BASE_URL=http://localhost:11434

# Longest reply the model may produce. 8192 by default.
# EDI_LLM_MAX_TOKENS=8192

# May the dropdown in the app change the model, and store keys on this
# machine? Yes when EDI is running for the person in front of it, no when
# EDI_LIMITS_ENABLED=1 says it is on a public URL -- because there the key
# would land on the operator's disk and the endpoint could be pointed
# anywhere. Set this to override the guess in either direction.
# EDI_ALLOW_MODEL_SWITCHING=1

# Where the Claude Code CLI lives, if it is not simply `claude` on PATH.
# EDI_CLAUDE_CODE_BINARY=claude


# --- Ollama, where the machine is yours ------------------------------------
#
# All optional. Ollama decides these from hardware this app cannot see, and is
# usually right; they are here for when it is not. GET /api/health reports
# whichever are set, under llm_config.runtime.

# Layers to put on the GPU. 0 forces CPU.
# EDI_OLLAMA_NUM_GPU=0

# CPU threads, for whatever is not on the GPU.
# EDI_OLLAMA_NUM_THREAD=8

# Context window. Raise it on a wide sheet: the SQL prompt carries the schema
# and sample rows, and a context too small to hold them is truncated from the
# front rather than refused -- so the model loses the schema and writes
# confident SQL against columns it can no longer see.
# EDI_OLLAMA_NUM_CTX=8192

# How long weights stay loaded between questions. Ollama unloads after five
# minutes and the reload is paid by whoever asks next.
# EDI_OLLAMA_KEEP_ALIVE=30m

# Installing a provider: backend/requirements.txt ships Google only. See
# backend/requirements-providers.txt for the one line to add for the others.


# --- Where workspaces are stored -------------------------------------------
#
# sqlite    a local file. No account, no keys. The default when no Supabase
#           service-role key is set.
# supabase  Postgres. Required on serverless, where the filesystem is
#           read-only and no two requests share an instance.
#
# Chosen automatically unless you say otherwise.
# EDI_STORE=sqlite

# Where the local store writes. Default: ./.edi-data/
# EDI_DATA_DIR=.edi-data

# Your Supabase project URL, e.g. https://xxxxxxxxxxxx.supabase.co
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=

# The Supabase *service role* key, from Project Settings -> API.
# This bypasses row-level security, so it must stay server-side. Never put it
# in a NEXT_PUBLIC_ variable: those are inlined into the browser bundle.
# Setting this is what selects the Supabase store.
# SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=


# --- Usage limits ----------------------------------------------------------
#
# Off unless you turn them on, because running EDI for yourself there is
# nobody to rate limit.
#
# Turn them on before putting this anywhere the public can reach: there is no
# sign-up, so every visitor is anonymous, and every question is a model call
# charged to you. The numbers below are sized for a handful of people trying
# out a link; raise them for anything with real users.
# EDI_LIMITS_ENABLED=1

# Questions allowed per visitor per rolling window, and the window in seconds.
# EDI_BURST_CALLS=5
# EDI_BURST_WINDOW_SECONDS=60

# Questions per visitor per day, and across everyone per day. The second one
# is the ceiling on what a deployment can spend; it is the limit that cannot
# be sidestepped by rotating IPs or clearing browser storage.
# EDI_DAILY_CALLS_PER_VISITOR=50
# EDI_DAILY_CALLS_TOTAL=1000

# Payload caps.
# EDI_MAX_QUESTION_CHARS=2000
# EDI_MAX_UPLOAD_BYTES=4194304
# EDI_MAX_ROWS=20000
# EDI_MAX_COLUMNS=100



# --- Serving the two halves from different domains -------------------------
#
# Leave this unset if the browser reaches the API on the same origin it was
# served from -- behind a reverse proxy, or on a platform that routes /api/*
# here, or through `next dev`. Same-origin requests are not subject to CORS, so
# there is nothing to configure and nothing exposed.
#
# Set it when the frontend and backend are on different domains, naming the
# origins the browser will be on. A wildcard is refused: it would let any page
# on the internet call your API, including the endpoints that spend model
# calls.
# EDI_CORS_ORIGINS=https://edi.example.com,https://staging.example.com


# --- Read by the frontend, not the backend ---------------------------------
#
# These two go in edi-frontend/.env.local, or in the frontend's environment on
# whatever you deploy to -- not in this file. Neither is a NEXT_PUBLIC_
# variable, so neither is inlined into the browser bundle.
#
# BACKEND_ORIGIN tells next.config.ts to proxy /api/* to the backend, which
# makes development same-origin the way production is:
#
#   BACKEND_ORIGIN=http://127.0.0.1:8000
#
# EDI_DOCS_ONLY=1 redirects /app to the documentation. It is what the hosted
# site at edi-gamma.vercel.app sets: the site is the docs, and the app is the
# repository you are reading them to set up. Unset -- the default, and what a
# clone gives you -- /app serves the whole application:
#
#   EDI_DOCS_ONLY=1
